---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

// Absolute, base-aware asset path. A relative "./car.png" resolves to /car.png
// if the page is ever served as /comp4020-ass1-hemansii without the trailing
// slash, which 404s on Pages; BASE_URL pins it to the repo subpath.
const carImage = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/car.png`;
---

<Layout title="F1 wing angle explainer">
  <nav aria-label="Skip navigation">
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to the explainer</a>
  </nav>
  <header>
    <h1>How much wing does an F1 car need?</h1>
    <p>
      Tilting the rear wing steeper sounds like it should just make the car
      faster. It doesn't. A steeper wing presses the car down onto the track,
      so it grips harder through corners. But it also catches more air, which
      holds the car back and costs top speed on the straights. Tilt it too far
      and the air stops flowing cleanly over the wing, so you lose the grip
      and the straight-line speed together.
    </p>
  </header>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="car-section" aria-labelledby="car-heading">
      <h2 id="car-heading">What the air over the wing is doing</h2>

      <div class="car-panel">
        <div class="car-scene" data-testid="car-scene">
          <img
            class="car-image"
            src={carImage}
            width="2720"
            height="1104"
            alt="Side view of a Formula 1 car, rear wing at the left"
          />
          <svg class="downforce-layer" viewBox="0 0 2720 830" aria-hidden="true">
            <path class="downforce-arrow" data-testid="downforce-arrow-1"></path>
            <path class="downforce-arrow" data-testid="downforce-arrow-2"></path>
          </svg>
          <svg class="airflow-layer" viewBox="0 0 2720 830" aria-hidden="true">
            <defs>
              <linearGradient id="airflow-fade" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="2650" x2="2100">
                <stop offset="0" stop-color="currentColor" stop-opacity="0"></stop>
                <stop offset="1" stop-color="currentColor" stop-opacity="1"></stop>
              </linearGradient>
            </defs>
            <path class="airflow-streak bloom" data-testid="airflow-bloom" stroke="url(#airflow-fade)"></path>
            <path class="airflow-streak core" data-testid="airflow-streak" stroke="url(#airflow-fade)"></path>
          </svg>
        </div>

        <div class="car-controls">
          <div class="slider-row">
            <span class="slider-end-label" aria-hidden="true">0°</span>
            {/* The label opens with the visible label's exact words so the
                accessible name still contains what's on screen (WCAG 2.5.3).
                The live value goes in aria-valuetext, set by interaction.ts:
                that is the attribute screen readers re-read on every change,
                whereas a name that changes underneath the user is not. */}
            <input
              type="range"
              id="wing-angle"
              data-testid="wing-angle-slider"
              aria-label="Drag to adjust wing angle. Sets the rear wing's tilt from 0 to 30 degrees, which drives downforce, drag, corner speed and top speed."
              min="0"
              max="30"
              step="1"
              value="9"
            />
            <span class="slider-end-label" aria-hidden="true">30°</span>
          </div>
          <label for="wing-angle" class="slider-label">Drag to adjust wing angle</label>

          {/* Shortcuts to two slider positions, not a second control: they
              dispatch input on the slider itself, so the car, chart and stats
              answer them exactly as they answer a drag. */}
          <div class="preset-row">
            <span class="preset-row-label">Or jump to a real setup</span>
            <button
              type="button"
              class="preset"
              data-testid="preset-monza"
              aria-label="Monza: 3 degrees, the least wing, for top speed down the straights"
            >Monza</button>
            <button
              type="button"
              class="preset"
              data-testid="preset-monaco"
              aria-label="Monaco: 17 degrees, near the most wing the flow will still hold, for grip through the corners"
            >Monaco</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="car-caption">
        The wing stays at the angle it was drawn at, so what the slider changes
        is the air. Below the stall angle the flow stays smooth and attached to
        the wing. At or past it the flow lets go, tumbles, and the wing stops
        working.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="chart-section" aria-labelledby="chart-heading">
      <h2 id="chart-heading">Corner speed and top speed vs. wing angle</h2>

      {/* Axis labels live outside the SVG rather than in it: as real text they
          take the page's own font and rem sizing directly, and the plot's
          geometry, gradient coordinates and height all stay untouched. */}
      <div class="chart-frame">
        <span class="axis-label axis-label-y" aria-hidden="true">Speed (km/h)</span>
        <svg class="angle-chart" viewBox="0 0 600 210" data-testid="angle-chart" aria-hidden="true">
          <defs>
            {/* Both ramps run across the same plot extents (x 12..588 = 0..30
                deg), so "dark at flat wing, light at full wing" reads identically
                on the two lines. Set as an attribute rather than in CSS: a
                url(#id) in a stylesheet resolves its fragment against the
                stylesheet, not the document. */}
            <linearGradient id="corner-ramp" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="12" x2="588">
              <stop offset="0" stop-color="#207974"></stop>
              <stop offset="1" stop-color="#82ede8"></stop>
            </linearGradient>
            <linearGradient id="top-speed-ramp" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="12" x2="588">
              <stop offset="0" stop-color="#a56f12"></stop>
              <stop offset="1" stop-color="#fcd87e"></stop>
            </linearGradient>
          </defs>
          <line class="stall-line" data-testid="stall-line"></line>
          <text class="stall-annotation" data-testid="stall-annotation">Stall: past this point, you lose both</text>
          <path class="corner-speed-path" data-testid="corner-speed-path" stroke="url(#corner-ramp)"></path>
          <path class="top-speed-path" data-testid="top-speed-path" stroke="url(#top-speed-ramp)"></path>
          <line class="marker-line" data-testid="angle-marker-line"></line>
          <circle class="corner-speed-marker" data-testid="corner-speed-marker" r="4"></circle>
          <circle class="top-speed-marker" data-testid="top-speed-marker" r="4"></circle>
        </svg>
        <span class="axis-label axis-label-x" aria-hidden="true">Wing angle (°)</span>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-meta">
        <p class="chart-legend">
          <span class="legend-item corner">Corner speed</span>
          <span class="legend-item top">Top speed</span>
        </p>

        <p class="best-lap-line">
          Best lap time found: <output for="wing-angle" data-testid="best-lap-value" class="best-lap-value"
          ></output>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="stall-insight" data-testid="stall-insight" hidden>
        This is why every team runs a different wing angle per track. Monaco
        needs corner grip, Monza needs top speed, and neither wants to go past
        stall.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* Each card is the live region rather than the number inside it, and
        aria-atomic makes the whole card the announcement, so a screen reader
        says "Downforce, 4253 N" instead of a bare "4253 N" with no clue which
        of the four stats just moved. The values are plain spans for the same
        reason: <output> carries an implicit role="status", which would nest a
        second live region inside this one and win the announcement back. */}
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        <span class="stat-label">Downforce</span>
        <span data-testid="downforce-value" class="stat-value"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        <span class="stat-label">Drag coefficient</span>
        <span data-testid="drag-value" class="stat-value"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        <span class="stat-label">Corner speed</span>
        <span data-testid="corner-speed-value" class="stat-value"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        <span class="stat-label">Top speed</span>
        <span data-testid="top-speed-value" class="stat-value"></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="model-notes">
      <summary>The numbers behind this</summary>
      <div class="model-notes-body">
        <p>
          Four relationships produce every number on this page. None of them is
          tuned to a particular car: they are the standard textbook shapes, and
          they are what make the trade-off come out the way it does.
        </p>

        <dl class="model-relations">
          <div>
            <dt>Downforce</dt>
            <dd>
              Tilting the wing steeper makes it work harder, so downforce climbs
              roughly in proportion to the angle. That holds only up to the stall
              angle. Past it the flow separates, and the wing makes <em>less</em>
              downforce however much further you tilt it.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Drag</dt>
            <dd>
              Downforce is not free. The drag it adds grows with the
              <em>square</em> of the downforce, so the cost accelerates away from
              you: roughly speaking, doubling the load on the wing quadruples the
              drag penalty. Past stall the separated flow keeps drag climbing even
              as downforce falls, which is why you lose both at once.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Corner speed</dt>
            <dd>
              Grip is friction acting on everything pressing the car into the
              road: its own weight, plus the wing's downforce. The speed a corner
              will take rises with the <em>square root</em> of that load, so the
              returns diminish. Four times the load buys only twice the speed.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Top speed</dt>
            <dd>
              On a straight the car settles at the speed where all the engine's
              power is spent shoving air aside. That puts top speed on the
              <em>cube root</em> of power divided by drag, so a small gain in
              top speed costs a large cut in drag.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <p>
          <strong>The constants are illustrative.</strong> Engine power, wing
          area, tyre grip, corner radius and the drag coefficients are numbers
          chosen to produce a realistic-feeling car, not telemetry measured from
          a real one. What is meant to be right is the shape of the curves and
          where they cross, not any single figure.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>How it compares.</strong> Real F1 cars top out around 280 to
          340 km/h, and take medium-speed corners around 140 to 210 km/h,
          depending on the circuit and the setup. This model spans 258 to 331
          km/h on the straight and 143 to 206 km/h through the corner, so the
          corner range sits inside the real one while the top speeds sit at the
          low end of it. Its draggiest wing settings are steeper than anything a
          team would actually run.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </main>
  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>Built for COMP4020 at the Australian National University.</p>
  </footer>
  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
